  "enhanced_information": "The virus with an envelope composed of nuclear membrane molecules suggests it may replicate in the nucleus and utilize host cellular machinery. Among the options, the Shingles virus (Varicella-Zoster Virus), a herpesvirus, is known to have nuclear membrane components and can cause painful rashes and blisters. However, the Ebola virus, which also has an envelope and can cause hemorrhagic fever, is more closely associated with systemic infections and has a well-documented nuclear interaction. Erythema infectiosum (Parvovirus B19) and Condylomata acuminata (HPV) do not have envelopes derived from nuclear membranes, making them less likely. Yellow fever virus, an arbovirus, also does not have nuclear membrane components. Therefore, the most likely virus that has infected the cell sample is the Ebola virus, which can cause hemorrhagic fever."
